COMPILER(1)                KERNEL COMPILER FUNCTIONS                COMPILER(1)NAME       compiler - kernel compiler support functionsSYNOPSIS       compiler ( -- )       (branch) ( -- ) compilation        (?branch) ( flag -- ) compilation       (do) ( end start -- ) compilation       (?do) ( end start -- ) compilation       (loop) ( -- ) compilation       (+loop) ( increment -- ) compilation       (literal) ( -- literal) compilation       (.") ( -- string) compilation       (abort") ( flag -- ) compilation       (;) ( -- ) compilation       (does>) ( -- ) compilation       thread ( ptr -- )        unthread ( ptr1 -- ptr2)       >mark ( -- marker) compilation       <mark ( -- marker) compilation       >resolve ( marker -- ) compilation       <resolve ( marker -- ) compilationDESCRIPTION       Kernel compiler definitions isolated to a separate vocabulary        to minimize dependencies and increase portability.compiler ( -- )       Vocabulary containing the compilation word set. Compiler        support, compiled words and threading primitives.(branch) ( -- ) compilation        Performs the run-time action of branching in threaded code.        Literal succeeding this word contains a relative address to        the next thread. (?branch) ( flag -- ) compilation       Performs the run-time action of conditionally branching in        threaded code. If the flag is zero a branch is performed       using a literal succeeding this word else the literal is        skipped. (do) ( end start -- ) compilation       Performs the run-time action of initializing for a loop block.        Moves the two parameters, "end" and "start", to the return        stack and a pointer to the relative address literal succeeding       this word so that "leave" may be performed in any section of       the looped block.(?do) ( end start -- ) compilation       Performs the run-time action of initializing for a loop block if       the "start" and "end" index are not equal else skips the block.       Moves the two parameters, "end" and "start", to the return        stack and a pointer to the relative address literal succeeding       this word so that "leave" may be performed in any section of       the looped block.(loop) ( -- ) compilation       Performs the run-time action of incrementing the loop index and       checking it against the upper limit for the loop. If the index       is still within the loop limit a branch is performed back to the        beginning of the loop. The branch offset is stored in-line        directly after the "(loop)" thread.(+loop) ( increment -- ) compilation       Performs the same action as "(loop)" but uses the top of stack       value as the increment of the loop index. Checks if the loop       limit has been exceeded.(literal) ( -- literal) compilation       Pushes the in-line constant following the threaded code onto       the parameter stack.(.") ( -- string) compilation       Pushes the in-line string pointer following the threaded code       onto the parameter stack.(abort") ( flag -- ) compilation       Performs the run-time action of checking the error flag and       if non-zero, true, aborts the current computation. Uses an       in-line string to display an abort message.(;) ( -- ) compilation       Performs the run-time action of returning from a colon definition.(does>) ( -- ) compilation       Performs the run-time action of returning from the current colon       definition and setting the code type of the last defined entry       to be the code following "(does>)" thus giving it the right       run-time action.thread ( ptr -- )        Used to create threaded code in the dictionary area. Allows code       to be written without considering the threading principle.unthread ( ptr1 -- ptr2)       Given a reference to threaded code returns an unthreaded entry       pointer.>mark ( -- marker) compilation       Allocates space for a branch offset and marks it for forward       resolving.<mark ( -- marker) compilation       Marks the current position in the compilation code stream as       a position for backwards branch.>resolve ( marker -- ) compilation       Resolves a forward branch offset.<resolve ( marker -- ) compilation       Allocates space for and calculates a backward branch offset.SEE ALSO       forth(1)WARNING       Code written using this word set is not directly portable to       other forth environments.BUG       Pointers are not check. Illegal pointer value will result in       a possible error situation.     COPYING       Copyright (C) 1989 Mikael R.K. Patel       Permission is granted to make and distribute verbatim copies       of this manual provided the copyright notice and this permission       notice are preserved on all copies.       Permission is granted to copy and distribute modified versions       of this manual under the conditions for verbatim copying,        provided also that the section entitled "GNU General Public       License" is included exactly as in the original, and provided       that the entire resulting derived work is distributed under       the terms of a permission notice identical to this one.       Permission is granted to copy and distribute translations of       this manual into another language, under the above conditions       for modified versions, except that the section entitled "GNU       General Public License" may be included in a translation approved       by the author instead of in the original English.AUTHOR       Mikael R.K. Patel       Computer Aided Design Laboratory (CADLAB)       Department of Computer and Information Science       Linkoping University       S-581 83 LINKOPING       SWEDEN       Email: mip@ida.liu.se